@anon.6e01a69b2c234dfdc5e23dbc943ea0bc.1214 = private unnamed_addr constant <{ [1017 x i8] }> <{ [1017 x i8] c"Creates a new radial gradient, in which colors radiate away from an\0Aorigin.\0A\0AThe gradient is defined by two circles: the focal circle and the end\0Acircle. The focal circle is a circle with center `focal-center` and\0Aradius `focal-radius`, that defines the points at which the gradient\0Astarts and has the color of the first stop. The end circle is a circle\0Awith center `center` and radius `radius`, that defines the points at\0Awhich the gradient ends and has the color of the last stop. The gradient\0Ais then interpolated between these two circles.\0A\0AUsing these four values, also called the focal point for the starting\0Acircle and the center and radius for the end circle, we can define a\0Agradient with more interesting properties than a basic radial gradient.\0A\0A```example\0A>>> #set circle(radius: 30pt)\0A#stack(\0A  dir: ltr,\0A  spacing: 1fr,\0A  circle(fill: gradient.radial(\0A    ..color.map.viridis,\0A  )),\0A  circle(fill: gradient.radial(\0A    ..color.map.viridis,\0A    focal-center: (10%, 40%),\0A    focal-radius: 5%,\0A  )),\0A)\0A```" }>, align 1
